@startuml model_refresh_decision
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' model_refresh_decision — TaskListModel::refresh() (v20.1).
' Converted from Mermaid in the v26.8 audit: this diagram was indexed as
' canonical but had never been rendered once, so nobody had actually seen it.
'
' The lesson it carries: a QAbstractItemModel has three ways to say "things
' changed", and they are ranked by how much of the user's context they
' destroy. dataChanged keeps scroll AND selection; insert/remove keeps
' scroll mostly; reset throws both away. So you spend effort proving you
' may use the cheap one, and fall back honestly when you cannot.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!include _style.puml

title TaskListModel::refresh() — spend a diff to save the user's scroll position

start
:AppData::changed() fires;
:take a fresh snapshot,
diff it against the old one;

if (same ids, same order?) then (yes)
  :<b>dataChanged</b> on rows whose
  <i>visible role</i> actually moved;
  note right
    A description-only edit emits NOTHING:
    the description is not a role this view
    paints. Scroll and selection both survive.
  end note
else (no)
  if (do the survivors keep\ntheir relative order?) then (yes)
    :<b>beginRemoveRows</b> (vanished ids)
    + <b>beginInsertRows</b> (new ids)
    + dataChanged (edited survivors);
    note right
      Scroll mostly survives; selection
      follows the rows Qt can track.
    end note
  else (no — a due-date edit re-sorted a row)
    :<b>beginResetModel</b>;
    note right
      The honest fallback: assume everything
      moved. Cheap to write, expensive to the
      user — which is exactly why it is last.
    end note
  endif
endif

stop
@enduml
